Answer:
Having an adequate amount of folic acid in a woman's body before pregnancy can help prevent major birth defects that affect her infant's brain and spine. These birth defects are called neural tube defects (NTDs). Women need to take folic acid every day, even in the period before pregnancy.
It is taken with or without food, and to reduce stomach upset, it can be taken after eating.
In the suspension form, the container must be shaken well before use. Minor side effects may occur, such as:
Nausea and loss of appetite;
Bloating, gas;
Bitter taste in the mouth;
Sleep problems;
Depression, or
Feeling excited or irritable.
